---
.title = "Dialects",
.description = "Add your own syntax to JavaScript, TypeScript, and JSX, and reuse the rest of Yuku.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-10-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
A dialect adds your own syntax to JavaScript, TypeScript, or JSX, from a single operator to a whole language such as [TSRX](https://tsrx.dev). You write only the new syntax, in one Zig file. Yuku parses, analyzes, walks, and prints the rest of the language to the spec, and your nodes with it.

## [How it works]($section.id('how-it-works'))

The `yuku` npm package runs Yuku as one WebAssembly module and decodes its output, the AST and the semantic model, into JavaScript objects. A dialect is compiled into Yuku itself, which makes your own WebAssembly module: Yuku with your syntax in its parser. Pass it to `yuku` as the `wasm` option, and `parse`, `analyze`, `walk`, and `generate` work as before, with your nodes in the tree next to standard ones. Standard code parses and analyzes exactly as in Yuku.

## [Example]($section.id('example'))

This dialect adds a pipeline operator, `|>`, with `%` as the value piped in.

```zig
// src/pipeline.zig
const std = @import("std");
const yuku = @import("parser");

const ast = yuku.ast;
const dialect = yuku.dialect;

pub const files: []const dialect.File = &.{.{ .extension = ".pipe.js", .lang = .js }};

pub const Node = enum {
    pipeline,
    topic,

    pub fn shape(node: Node) dialect.Shape {
        return switch (node) {
            .pipeline => .{ .type = "PipelineExpression", .fields = &.{ "left", "right" } },
            .topic => .{ .type = "TopicReference" },
        };
    }
};

pub fn operator(
    p: *dialect.Parser,
    left: ast.NodeIndex,
    precedence: u8,
) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim {
    if (!std.mem.startsWith(u8, p.source()[p.token().span.start..], "|>")) return .decline;
    if (precedence > ast.Precedence.Assignment) return .end;

    if (!try p.advance() or !try p.advance()) return .failed;
    const right = try p.parseExpression(ast.Precedence.Assignment + 1) orelse return .failed;
    const span: ast.Span = .{ .start = p.tree().span(left).start, .end = p.tree().span(right).end };
    return .{ .node = try p.addNode(.pipeline, &.{ left, right }, span) };
}

pub fn expression(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim {
    const token = p.token();
    if (token.tag != .percent) return .decline;

    if (!try p.advance()) return .failed;
    return .{ .node = try p.addNode(.topic, &.{}, token.span) };
}

pub fn print(w: *dialect.Printer, kind: Node, children: []const ast.NodeIndex) dialect.Error!void {
    switch (kind) {
        .pipeline => {
            try w.node(children[0]);
            try w.write(" |> ");
            try w.node(children[1]);
        },
        .topic => try w.write("%"),
    }
}
```

With Yuku as a [dependency](/#zig), build it into a WebAssembly module.

```zig
// build.zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn build(b: *std.Build) void {
    const yuku = b.dependency("yuku", .{ .dialect = b.path("src/pipeline.zig") });
    b.installArtifact(@import("yuku").addWasm(b, yuku, "pipeline"));
}
```

`zig build` writes `zig-out/bin/pipeline.wasm`. Use it from JavaScript.

```js
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { parse } from "yuku";

const wasm = new WebAssembly.Module(readFileSync("zig-out/bin/pipeline.wasm"));
const { program } = parse("const total = prices |> sum(%) |> round(%, 2);", {
  path: "app.pipe.js",
  wasm,
});
```

The pipelines group left to right, and each decodes as `{ type: "PipelineExpression", start, end, left, right }`. The calls inside them are standard nodes, so `analyze` resolves `sum` and `round`, and `walk` visits everything. `generate` prints the line back as written once its [`print`](/parser/codegen/#js-printing-new-nodes) option covers your nodes.

## [The dialect file]($section.id('the-dialect-file'))

A dialect is a Zig file with these public declarations. It imports the parser it is compiled into as `@import("parser")`.

| Declaration        | Required            | Purpose                                                       |
| ------------------ | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `files`            | yes                 | The file extensions your syntax lives in, see [Files](#files) |
| `Node`             | to add nodes        | Your node kinds and their shapes, see [Nodes](#nodes)         |
| Position functions | at least one        | Where your syntax starts, see [Positions](#positions)         |
| `print`            | to print your nodes | How codegen prints them, see [Printing](#printing)            |

Mistakes are compile errors: a missing `files`, a function that takes a `*dialect.Parser` but is not named after a position, a wrong signature, a `Node` without `shape`, or an invalid shape.

## [Files]($section.id('files'))

`files` lists the kinds of file your syntax lives in. Each entry is a `dialect.File`, an `extension` and the standard `lang` it extends: `.js`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, `.tsx`, or `.dts`.

```zig
pub const files: []const dialect.File = &.{
    .{ .extension = ".pipe.js", .lang = .js },
    .{ .extension = ".pipe.ts", .lang = .ts },
};
```

- In JavaScript, a source whose `path` ends in a listed extension parses with the dialect, in that entry's `lang`. A source with no `path` uses the first entry. Any other path, or an explicit `lang` option, parses as standard syntax. One build reads a whole project.
- In Zig, a parse opts in with the `dialect` option: `parser.parse(allocator, source, .{ .dialect = true })`.

## [Positions]($section.id('positions'))

A position is a point in the grammar where the parser asks your dialect first, by calling the `pub fn` of that name. It parses your syntax there, or declines and standard parsing continues.

| Position           | Called at                                                                 |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `statement`        | The start of every statement                                              |
| `expression`       | The start of every operand, before any prefix operator                    |
| `operator`         | After the operand `left`, where an infix or postfix operator could follow |
| `function_body`    | Where a function, method, or arrow function body starts                   |
| `jsx_child`        | The start of each JSX child, at byte `offset` in `parent`                 |
| `jsx_attribute`    | A `{` in a JSX tag, where standard JSX reads a spread attribute           |
| `jsx_element_name` | The start of an element name, in an opening or closing tag                |
| `module_specifier` | A module specifier that is not a string                                   |

```zig
pub fn statement(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn expression(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn operator(p: *dialect.Parser, left: ast.NodeIndex, precedence: u8) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn function_body(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn jsx_child(p: *dialect.Parser, offset: u32, parent: ast.NodeIndex) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn jsx_attribute(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn jsx_element_name(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
pub fn module_specifier(p: *dialect.Parser) dialect.Error!dialect.Claim
```

Each returns a `dialect.Claim`.

| Claim                | Means                                                          |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `.decline`           | Not your syntax. Consume no tokens and add no nodes.           |
| `.{ .node = index }` | The node you parsed, with the current token just past it.      |
| `.failed`            | A diagnostic was reported, by you or by a parse call.          |
| `.end`               | `operator` only. The expression ends before the current token. |

- Positions apply everywhere, including inside your own nodes. A `parseExpression` inside your node asks your `expression` and `operator` again, so your syntax and standard syntax nest.
- Decline anything the standard grammar accepts, unless you mean to read it differently.
- `operator` receives the lowest `precedence` that continues the current expression. When your operator binds more loosely, answer `.end` so an enclosing expression takes it, as the example does.
- `function_body` allows `return` and a directive prologue inside, as a block body does.
- A `jsx_child` ends on its last token, which stays current, since the parser reads the text after it by offset. `parent` is the opening element or fragment. Use `seek(offset)` when the child starts with tokens.

## [The parser]($section.id('the-parser'))

A position function drives the parser through a `*dialect.Parser`.

| Call                                 | Does                                                                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `token()`                            | The current token, an `ast.Token` with a `tag` and a byte `span`.                                                       |
| `advance()`                          | Moves to the next token. Returns `false` after a lexical error, which is already reported.                              |
| `expect(tag, message)`               | Consumes a token of `tag`, or reports `message` and returns `false`.                                                    |
| `tokenAt(offset)`                    | The token at or after a byte offset, without moving the parser. For lookahead.                                          |
| `source()`                           | The source text.                                                                                                        |
| `seek(offset)`                       | Makes the token at a byte offset current. For a JSX child that starts with tokens.                                      |
| `checkpoint()`, `rewind(checkpoint)` | Saves the position and returns to it, to try a parse and undo it.                                                       |
| `semicolon(end)`                     | Ends a statement at `end`, with a `;` or automatic semicolon insertion. Null after a diagnostic.                        |
| `report(span, message)`              | Reports an error.                                                                                                       |
| `parseStatement()`                   | Parses a statement.                                                                                                     |
| `parseExpression(precedence)`        | Parses an expression whose operators bind at `precedence` or tighter. `ast.Precedence.Lowest` reads a whole expression. |
| `parseBindingPattern()`              | Parses an identifier, array, or object pattern, with its type annotation in TypeScript.                                 |
| `parseJsxExpressionContainer()`      | Parses the `{ expression }` at the current `{` inside a tag.                                                            |
| `parseJsxElement()`                  | Parses the element or fragment at the current `<`, leaving its last `>` current.                                        |
| `addNode(kind, children, span)`      | Adds one of your nodes, see [Nodes](#nodes).                                                                            |
| `list()`                             | Collects children of unknown count, see below.                                                                          |
| `addJsxText(span)`                   | Adds the source text in `span` as JSX text.                                                                             |
| `addComment(type, span)`             | Records a comment where the standard grammar reads none, such as inside text your syntax owns.                          |
| `tree()`                             | The `ast.Tree`, to read nodes and build standard ones, see [Building and editing](/parser/ast/#building-and-editing).   |

A parse call returns `null` after it reports a diagnostic. Answer `.failed` then.

`list()` collects children on the parser's scratch space, for nodes with a variable number of children.

```zig
var children = p.list();
defer children.reset();
while (p.token().tag != .right_brace) {
    try children.append(try p.parseStatement() orelse return .failed);
}
const node = try p.addNode(.block, children.items(), span);
```

## [Nodes]($section.id('nodes'))

Your node kinds are a `Node` enum. Its `shape` function describes each kind.

```zig
pub const Node = enum {
    each,

    pub fn shape(node: Node) dialect.Shape {
        return switch (node) {
            .each => .{
                .type = "EachStatement",
                .fields = &.{ "item", "list", "body[]" },
                .block_scope = true,
                .lexical_bindings = true,
            },
        };
    }
};
```

| Field              | Default | Means                                                                                                        |
| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `type`             |         | The node's `type` in JavaScript.                                                                             |
| `fields`           | `&.{}`  | The names of its children, in order. One name may end in `[]`, and takes the children left over as an array. |
| `properties`       | `"{}"`  | A JSON object of constant properties every node of the kind has in JavaScript.                               |
| `block_scope`      | `false` | The node opens a block scope.                                                                                |
| `lexical_bindings` | `false` | Each binding identifier inside the node declares a `let`-like binding in the current scope.                  |
| `catch_bindings`   | `false` | Each binding identifier inside the node declares a catch parameter, as in `catch (e) { var e }`.             |
| `iteration`        | `false` | The node is a loop, which `break` and `continue` inside it end or skip.                                      |

`addNode(kind, children, span)` adds a node. `children` follow `fields` in order, with `.null` for an absent part, and lie inside `span`. In Zig, the node is an `ast.Dialect`, with its `kind` and its `children` in source order, which `tree.extra(node.children)` reads.

In JavaScript, a node is a plain object: its `type`, `start` and `end`, each field, and the `properties`. With the shape above, `each (item of list) { ... }` decodes as `{ type: "EachStatement", start, end, item, list, body: [...] }`, and `item` is bound in the statement's own scope.

## [Printing]($section.id('printing'))

An optional `print` writes your nodes back to source. Without it, codegen reports a diagnostic for each of your nodes.

```zig
pub fn print(w: *dialect.Printer, kind: Node, children: []const ast.NodeIndex) dialect.Error!void
```

| Call           | Does                                              |
| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| `write(code)`  | Writes code, with a space before it where needed. |
| `space()`      | Writes a space in the pretty format.              |
| `node(index)`  | Prints any node, standard or yours.               |
| `block(items)` | Prints a list of statements between braces.       |
| `tree()`       | The tree, to read nodes.                          |

In JavaScript, `yuku/codegen` prints your nodes through its [`print`](/parser/codegen/#js-printing-new-nodes) option, with the same calls.

## [Analysis]($section.id('analysis'))

Semantic analysis walks into your nodes like any other. Standard code inside them declares, resolves, and reports early errors as usual, and names inside them resolve to the bindings around them. The [shape](#nodes) decides the scope and bindings your node adds, and whether it is a loop.

## [Walking and lowering]($section.id('walking-and-lowering'))

In Zig, every [traverser](/parser/traverse/) mode walks your nodes, with `enter_dialect` and `exit_dialect` hooks that receive the `ast.Dialect`.

```zig
pub fn enter_dialect(self: *Lower, node: ast.Dialect, index: ast.NodeIndex, ctx: *transform.Ctx) traverser.Action
pub fn exit_dialect(self: *Lower, node: ast.Dialect, index: ast.NodeIndex, ctx: *transform.Ctx) void
```

To compile your syntax to standard JavaScript, replace each of your nodes with standard ones in a [transform](/parser/traverse/#transform) pass, with `ctx.tree.setData(index, ...)` and the tree's building calls. Every tool then reads plain JavaScript.

## [Building]($section.id('building'))

Pass the dialect file to the `yuku` dependency as `dialect`.

```zig
const yuku = b.dependency("yuku", .{
    .target = target,
    .optimize = optimize,
    .dialect = b.path("src/pipeline.zig"),
});
my_module.addImport("parser", yuku.module("parser"));
```

`addWasm(b, yuku, name)` adds the WebAssembly module for JavaScript, which `b.installArtifact` writes to `zig-out/bin/<name>.wasm`. Inside the Yuku repository, `zig build wasm -Ddialect=src/pipeline.zig` builds it as `zig-out/bin/yuku.wasm`.

A build holds one parser, and so one dialect.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

Pass your module as the `wasm` option of `parse`, `analyze`, or `new Analyzer`. [Files](#files) decides which sources parse with the dialect.

```js
const { program } = parse(source, { path: "app.pipe.js", wasm });
const module = analyze(source, { path: "app.pipe.js", wasm });
```

[`yuku/ast`](/parser/ast/#js-walking) walks your nodes by their fields, and visitors can name your types. [`yuku/analyzer`](/parser/semantic/#javascript) resolves the names inside them, and [`yuku/codegen`](/parser/codegen/#js-printing-new-nodes) prints them through its `print` option.

For TypeScript, declare your nodes in `DialectNodes`, which adds them to `Node`, so visitors and `node.type` checks know them.

```ts
import type { BaseNode, Expression } from "yuku/types";

declare module "yuku/types" {
  interface DialectNodes {
    PipelineExpression: BaseNode & { type: "PipelineExpression"; left: Expression; right: Expression };
    TopicReference: BaseNode & { type: "TopicReference" };
  }
}
```

### [Publishing a package]($section.id('publishing-a-package'))

A dialect's package depends on `yuku`, ships the WebAssembly module, and passes it for its users.

```ts
// src/index.ts
import { parse as parseYuku, type ParseOptions, type ParseResult } from "yuku";
import { analyze as analyzeYuku, type AnalyzeOptions, type Module } from "yuku/analyzer";
import { pipeline } from "#module";

export function parse(source: string, options: Omit<ParseOptions, "wasm"> = {}): ParseResult {
  return parseYuku(source, { ...options, wasm: pipeline });
}

export function analyze(source: string, options: Omit<AnalyzeOptions, "wasm"> = {}): Module {
  return analyzeYuku(source, { ...options, wasm: pipeline });
}
```

To run in every runtime with no setup, load the module as `yuku` does: inline its bytes, and import the file in Cloudflare Workers and Vercel Edge, which cannot compile WebAssembly from bytes.

```ts
// src/module/inline.ts
import bytes from "../../zig-out/bin/pipeline.wasm";

export const pipeline = new WebAssembly.Module(bytes);
```

```ts
// src/module/import.ts
import pipeline from "#wasm";

export { pipeline };
```

```json
"imports": {
  "#module": {
    "workerd": "./dist/module/import.js",
    "edge-light": "./dist/module/import.js",
    "default": "./dist/module/inline.js"
  }
}
```

Bundle with a bundler that inlines `.wasm` as bytes, such as tsdown with `loader: { ".wasm": "binary" }`. Keep the `#` imports external, rewrite `#wasm` to `../pipeline.wasm?module`, as `outputOptions: { paths: { "#wasm": "../pipeline.wasm?module" } }` does, and copy `pipeline.wasm` into `dist`. Cloudflare's and Vercel's bundlers load a `.wasm?module` import as WebAssembly but match the path as written, so `#wasm` cannot reach them through `imports`. TypeScript reads the `#module`, `#wasm`, and `.wasm` imports from declarations like `yuku`'s own [imports.d.ts](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku/blob/main/packages/yuku/core/module/imports.d.ts).

## [Cost]($section.id('cost'))

A build without a dialect has no dialect code, since every position sits behind a compile-time check. With one compiled in, a standard file pays one flag check per position, and a dialect file one call.
